{
 "id": "2014-lin-awareness",
 "name": "Awareness of Street Trees: Attention Restoration and Restorativeness",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: a between-subjects experiment on whether you must notice street trees for them to restore attention. The page randomly assigns each participant to one of four groups. No Trees sees five Taipei streetscapes without greenery. Minimal Awareness sees the same images with 30 ms flashes of the tree version between mirrored masks every 5 s. Moderate Awareness sees the streetscapes with simulated street trees. Heightened Awareness sees the same tree images plus the instruction to observe the plants closely. The images are the paper's Figure 1 (CC BY). Procedure as in the paper: a Digit Span Backward test (lengths 4–10, two trials each, scored as the last length repeated correctly before the first mistake), five images for 20 s each, the five-item Perceived Restorativeness Scale (0–6, item wording printed in the paper), the Digit Span Backward again, then a free recall of the plant species seen as a manipulation check. Analysis: paired t-tests of pre vs post per group, ANCOVA of the change by group with the pre-test as covariate, ANOVA of PRS. Original study: 138 Taiwanese undergraduates; attention fell in the No Trees group and rose most with heightened awareness.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online and individual instead of in a classroom with projected PowerPoint slides; answers are typed instead of written; (2) digit sequences are generated at random per trial (the paper does not print them), and the test stops at the first mistake because later trials do not change the score; a first-trial error scores 4, the paper's stated minimum; (3) flash frames are timed in the browser at about 30 ms (two screen frames) and their onsets are logged; (4) the five streets come in random order; (5) the instruction to type digits, the intro and the plant-recall wording are mine.",
 "author": "Lin, Tsai, Sullivan, Chang & Chang",
 "year": "2014",
